  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 318 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAs we move into the mid-week period, fairly strong ridging will \ncontinue to build and become more broad with time.  A downstream \ntrough axis will swing through the region late Wednesday and could \nfire off a few showers and storms.  PWATs will increase to around \n1.8 inches, but model soundings show very weak shear, and rather \npoor lapse rates, despite ample instability.  For now, the highest \nchances of storms looks to across southern Kentucky.  Highs on \nWednesday will be in the upper 80s with lows in the upper 60s.\n\nWestern CONUS ridging will hold sway on Thursday with downstream \ntroughing across eastern US.  A fairly strong shortwave trough axis \nwill move east across the Great Lakes and drive another frontal \nboundary through the region.  There may some slightly better \nconvergence along that boundary resulting in another round of \nshowers/storms moving through the region.  Highs on Thursday will be \nin the upper 80s to around 90 with lows in the mid 60s.\n\nBehind the frontal passage, a dry period of weather is expected for \nthe late week and into the weekend.  The upper trough axis over the \nregion is forecast to move off to the east while mid-level height \nrises push east into Ohio Valley from the Plains.  This will result \nin temperatures slightly warming through the weekend, though deep \nmoisture will remain south/southwest of the region, so humidity \nvalues will not be bad.  Highs on Friday will be in the upper 80s \nwith highs Saturday through Monday will be in the lower 90s, with \novernight lows in the upper 60s to around 70.",
